<main>
<header>
  <h1>Credit risk cut-off strategy</h1>
  <p class="sub">Can the lender approve more applicants without raising the bad rate, and where should the cut-off be?</p>
  <div class="meta">Prepared for the Head of Credit Policy · Data: Home Credit "Credit Risk Model Stability" (Kaggle 2024), {{ m.data.loans | num }} booked consumer loans,
  decisions {{ m.data.first_decision }} to {{ m.data.last_decision }} · Headline window: out-of-time test, January to March 2020 ({{ m.data.test_loans | num }} loans, bad rate {{ m.data.test_bad_rate | pct(2) }}) ·
  Money in the dataset's undisclosed currency units (CU) · Generated {{ today }} from <code>reports/metrics.json</code></div>
</header>

<section id="summary">
<h2 style="border-top:none;margin-top:10px">Executive summary</h2>
<p class="lead"><b>Request.</b> "The business wants a higher approval rate, but the bad rate must not go up. Is there room on the current cut-off? If we move it, who goes from declined to approved, who goes from approved to declined, and how much money is at stake?"</p>
<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ m.vs_today[CH].total_cost_change | pct(0) }}</div><div class="l">cost of wrong decisions, cost-based rule vs today's policy (approve every booked loan)</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ m.model_value.same_bad_rate.extra_approvals_pp | pp }}</div><div class="l">approvals at the same bad rate, challenger vs a rebuilt scorecard applying the cost-based rule (≈ {{ m.model_value.same_bad_rate.extra_approvals_per_month | num }} booked loans a month); after-the-fact comparison on booked test loans, requiring its own cut-off fixed in advance and its own test</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ m.room[CH].tail_bad_rate | pct }}</div><div class="l">bad rate of the {{ m.room[CH].declined_share | pct }} of today's approvals that the rule would decline (book: {{ m.data.test_bad_rate | pct(2) }})</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ m.three_tier.totals.reviews_per_month | num }}</div><div class="l">manual reviews a month on average, the team's full capacity (5% of each month's applications); {{ uncapped_reviews | num }} a month would be worth reviewing</div></div>
</div>
<ol>
  <li><b>Set the cut-off from what a wrong decision costs.</b> At the assumed {{ m.economics.loss_rate | pct(0) }} loss rate (exposure at default × loss given default), defaults imply {{ m.approve_all.credit_loss | money }} of modelled losses on today's booked portfolio; a wrongly declined good customer costs the product's margin
    ({{ m.economics.margin_by_product.CAL | pct(0) }} cash, {{ m.economics.margin_by_product.REL | pct(0) }} revolving, {{ m.economics.margin_by_product.COL | pct(0) }} point-of-sale). That puts the break-even PD at
    {{ m.economics.breakeven_by_product.CAL | pct }}, {{ m.economics.breakeven_by_product.REL | pct }} and {{ m.economics.breakeven_by_product.COL | pct }}. With the challenger model, the resulting rule cuts the total cost of wrong decisions by
    <b>{{ (-m.vs_today[CH].total_cost_change) | pct(0) }}</b> and modelled credit losses by <b>{{ (-m.vs_today[CH].credit_loss_change) | pct(0) }}</b> versus today's policy (approving every booked loan; a 0.5 cut-off does almost the same here), and lowers the
    bad rate of the approved book from {{ m.data.test_bad_rate | pct(2) }} to {{ m.loss_curve[CH].rule.bad_rate_approved | pct(2) }}: worth {{ m.room[CH].net_gain | money }} over the quarter (95% bootstrap interval
    {{ m.uncertainty.room_net_gain.low | money }} to {{ m.uncertainty.room_net_gain.high | money }}).</li>
  <li><b>On the current scorecard there is room to tighten, not to loosen.</b> Moving the cut-off only trades approvals for bad rate. On booked loans, a better model is one way to approve more at the same bad rate:
    against a rebuilt scorecard applying the same rule, the challenger approves <b>{{ m.model_value.same_bad_rate.extra_approvals_pp | pp }}</b> more applications at the scorecard's bad rate
    (95% interval {{ m.uncertainty.same_bad_rate_uplift_pp.low | pp }} to {{ m.uncertainty.same_bad_rate_uplift_pp.high | pp }}). At the same approval rate its modelled credit losses are
    <b>{{ (-m.model_value.same_approval_rate.credit_loss_change_pct) | pct(0) }}</b> lower. These comparisons were matched after the fact on booked test loans. Each needs its own cut-off fixed in advance and its own test. Approving applicants the lender rejected in the past cannot be evaluated from this data (they were never booked);
    a below-cut-off test of {{ m.test_design.below_cutoff_loans_needed | num }} approved loans (≈ {{ m.test_design.price_of_information | money }} of expected net losses on the declined tail's economics, likely more because today's rejects are probably riskier)
    would measure it. The exact one-sided 2.5% binomial test has at least 80% power at the target and passes if at most {{ m.test_design.below_cutoff_pass_max_defaults }} loans default ({{ m.test_design.below_cutoff_pass_max_rate | pct }}); it passes {{ m.test_design.below_cutoff_power_at_target | pct(0) }} of the time if the true rate is the 25%-lower target ({{ m.test_pass.swap_in_bad_rate | pct }}).
    If the true rate is at that target, about {{ "%.1f" | format(m.test_pass.new_approvals_per_decline) }} new approvals per declined tail loan, roughly +{{ m.test_pass.net_change_per_quarter | num }} more loans a quarter, could be funded. Expansion is sized on the measured rate.</li>
  <li><b>Route the grey zone to people, within capacity.</b> {{ m.three_tier.tiers[0].share | pct }} auto-approve, {{ m.three_tier.tiers[1].share | pct }} manual review, {{ m.three_tier.tiers[2].share | pct }} decline.
    The review tier adds {{ m.three_tier.uplift_vs_two_tier | money }} a quarter over a two-tier rule at {{ m.economics.review.bad_catch_rate | pct(0) }} catch, {{ m.economics.review.good_decline_rate | pct(0) }} good-decline and {{ m.economics.review.cost_per_case | num }} CU a review ({{ RG.low | money }} to {{ RG.high | money }} at catch rates of {{ RG.c_lo | pct(0) }} to {{ RG.c_hi | pct(0) }}; section 8). It is piloted separately outside the trial arms to measure the catch rate before the gain is counted.</li>
  <li><b>Guardrails need confirmation.</b> Dated histories were rebuilt as of the decision and clearly leaking fields dropped; some retained long-window snapshot summaries, measured about 14 days after the decision, still carry residual look-ahead. Approval rates are virtually equal by sex and the lowest age-band
    ratio is {{ "%.2f" | format(fa.approval_rate_ratio.min()) }}, above the four-fifths screen, but risk is under-predicted for men and ages 18-24 (ratios {{ "%.2f" | format(fs.loc['Male', 'calibration_ratio']) }} and {{ "%.2f" | format(fa.loc['18-24', 'calibration_ratio']) }}). Monitoring would track calibration and early delinquency, because in the COVID months the challenger's score distribution barely moved while the default rate halved.</li>
</ol>
<div class="fig">{{ F.loss }}</div>
<p class="note">Each line is one model. The far right is today's policy, which approved every loan in this book (a 0.5 cut-off lands in almost the same place). Moving left declines the riskiest applicants first; total cost (modelled credit losses on approved bads + margin given up on declined goods) falls until roughly the break-even region,
then rises again as good customers are turned away. The dotted line is the product-level cost-based rule, which beats any single cut-off.</p>
</section>

<section id="scope">
<h2>1 · Request and scope</h2>
<p><b>Decision:</b> which credit policy to run for new consumer-loan applications: keep the current scorecard and cut-off, move the cut-off, or switch to a new model with a cost-based cut-off and a three-tier process.
<b>Decision maker:</b> Head of Credit Policy. <b>Sign-off:</b> Model Risk (model change), Compliance (fair lending, decline reasons), Underwriting Operations (review capacity), Finance (cost assumptions).</p>
<div class="callout warn"><b>The constraint behind every number.</b> All {{ m.data.loans | num }} loans in the data were approved by the lender's historical policy; nobody it rejected is in the data. Every approval rate here is therefore a share of
<i>previously approved</i> applicants, and the bad rate of anyone the lender would newly approve is an assumption, not a measurement. The incumbent production model is not in the data either; a WOE logistic-regression scorecard
(the industry-standard application model) is rebuilt as the "champion" and compared with a LightGBM "challenger".</div>
<p>Time windows (by decision month, never random): train January to October 2019 · validation November to December 2019 (early stopping, calibration) · <b>out-of-time test January to March 2020 (headline numbers)</b> ·
monitoring April to October 2020 (after the April 2020 break; used to illustrate the monitoring plan).</p>
</section>

<section id="data">
<h2>2 · Data and quality audit</h2>
<p>Five source tables, read in place with DuckDB. Every check is a standalone SQL file in <code>sql/dq/</code>.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.dq_rows }}</div>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.dq_keys }}</div>
<div class="fig">{{ F.monthly }}</div>
<p class="note">The bad rate rose from {{ TR.first_rate | pct }} in January 2019 to a peak of {{ TR.peak_rate | pct }} in {{ TR.peak_month }} and halved from April 2020. Volume fell by about {{ TR.drop | pct(0) }} in April 2020
({{ TR.mar_n | num }} loans in March, {{ TR.apr_n | num }} in April) and was back to March's level by {{ TR.back_month }}; October 2020 covers 1 to 5 October only. Model building stops at March 2020; the later months illustrate the monitoring plan.</p>
<h3>Look-ahead leakage: the history snapshot was taken about 14 days after the decision</h3>
<p>Event dates run up to 14 days past the decision date, never more; the only later dates are scheduled future due dates (up to 47 days). Recounting each client's applications from the previous-applications table shows when the pre-computed fields were measured:</p>
<div class="fig">{{ F.offset }}</div>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.offset }}</div>
<div class="fig">{{ F.pit }}</div>
<p>Events dated after the decision (a rejection elsewhere a week later, a new delinquency) go with much higher default rates. These fields are not available at decision time and overstate offline performance. Dated histories were therefore rebuilt from
applications created <i>before</i> the decision date; short-window and "current status" snapshot fields, tax-authority fields (which arrive 14 days late), interest-rate and campaign fields (outputs of the lender's own decision
process) and columns introduced part-way through 2019 were dropped. Some retained long-window snapshot summaries, measured about 14 days after the decision, still carry residual look-ahead. Days-past-due maxima on contracts still open at the decision are reduced by 14 days and floored at zero, a guaranteed lower bound.</p>
<h3>What the leak would have done</h3>
<div class="fig">{{ F.ablation }}</div>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.leak_groups }}</div>
<p>The naive model's edge is larger among the {{ m.leakage.share_post_decision_activity | pct }} of clients who did something after the decision
({{ "%+.3f" | format(m.leakage.gini_post_activity_naive - m.leakage.gini_post_activity_pit) }} Gini) than elsewhere
({{ "%+.3f" | format(m.leakage.gini_no_activity_naive - m.leakage.gini_no_activity_pit) }}). Swapping in decision-time values where they can be rebuilt removes only part of the gap
(Gini {{ "%.3f" | format(m.leakage.gini_test_naive_decision_time) }}). In money, under the same cost-based rule:</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.leak_business }}</div>
<p class="note">The leak exists, but its effect on ranking is small here because most of the signal comes from long repayment histories. Most of the remaining gap comes from snapshot fields (current debt, current
days past due, short-window counts) that mix pre-decision information with up to 14 days of post-decision information and cannot be separated. They were dropped, at a cost of less than one Gini point and
a fraction of a percent of profit. Retained long-window snapshot summaries still carry residual look-ahead; the fix at source is to re-extract snapshot fields as of the decision date and test them again.</p>
</section>

<section id="features">
<h2>3 · Features</h2>
<p>{{ m.leakage.raw_columns_reviewed }} raw columns were reviewed one by one against written rules (point-in-time, stability, fair lending, duplication, coverage): {{ m.leakage.kept }} kept as provided,
{{ m.leakage.rebuilt }} rebuilt point-in-time, {{ m.leakage.dropped }} dropped and {{ m.leakage.raw_columns_reviewed - m.leakage.kept - m.leakage.rebuilt - m.leakage.dropped }} kept only to test fair lending (never model inputs), each with a reason in <code>docs/feature_inclusion_log.csv</code>. The result is {{ m.leakage.n_features }} features in six groups:
loan terms, affordability, internal repayment history, previous applications, credit-bureau enquiries and contact-detail sharing.</p>
<div class="fig">{{ F.iv }}</div>
<p><b>Fair-lending exclusions.</b> Sex, age, marital and family status, number of children, education, language, income type (it contains pension and disability categories) and addresses are never model inputs.
Exclusion does not remove proxies: the 90 inputs still predict the attributes below, so outcomes are tested by group (section 9). For age 50+, AUC is {{ "%.2f" | format(m.leakage.proxy_auc_age50) }} with all inputs; {{ "%.2f" | format(m.leakage.proxy_auc_age50_without['Years with current employer']) }} without years with current employer; {{ "%.2f" | format(m.leakage.proxy_auc_age50_without['Product type and share of past cash applications']) }} without product type and share of past cash applications; {{ "%.2f" | format(m.leakage.proxy_auc_age50_without['Loan size, payment and income (7 inputs)']) }} without the 7 loan-size, payment and income inputs.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.proxy }}</div>
</section>

<section id="models">
<h2>4 · Models</h2>
<p><b>Champion:</b> {{ m.models.scorecard_variables }}-variable WOE logistic-regression scorecard (monotonic optimal binning; variables selected by information value with a correlation filter; every coefficient has the expected sign;
scaled to points, 600 = odds of 50:1, +20 points doubles the odds). <b>Challenger:</b> LightGBM on all 90 inputs, early-stopped on the validation window. Both are Platt-calibrated on the validation window.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.models }}</div>
<p>The challenger ranks better in every out-of-sample window (out-of-time Gini {{ "%.3f" | format(m.models.gini_test_challenger) }} vs {{ "%.3f" | format(m.models.gini_test_champion) }}). Most of the gap comes from using
more information rather than from the algorithm: LightGBM restricted to the scorecard's {{ m.models.scorecard_variables }} variables reaches {{ "%.3f" | format(m.models.gini_test_lgbm_on_scorecard_vars) }}, and giving missing values their
own WOE does not help the scorecard ({{ "%.3f" | format(m.models.gini_test_scorecard_empirical_missing) }}).{% if m.models.gini_test_scorecard_more_vars is defined %} A scorecard refreshed with the variable cap lifted
({{ m.models.scorecard_more_vars_n }} variables) reaches {{ "%.3f" | format(m.models.gini_test_scorecard_more_vars) }}.{% endif %}</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.champion_checks }}</div>
<p>The challenger's in-sample (train) Gini ({{ "%.3f" | format(gini_train_ch) }}) is much higher than its validation Gini ({{ "%.3f" | format(gini_valid_ch) }}), the usual gap for boosted trees;
the validation and out-of-time numbers agree, which is what matters. In money on the test window:</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.biz }}</div>
<div class="fig">{{ F.calibration }}</div>
<p class="note">Both models were calibrated on November to December 2019 (two months, bad rate {{ m.models.bad_rate_valid | pct(2) }}) and tested on January to March 2020 ({{ m.models.bad_rate_test | pct(2) }}): the ranking held, the level did not (challenger calibration ratio
{{ "%.2f" | format(m.models.calibration_ratio_test_challenger) }}, PDs about {{ (1 - m.models.calibration_ratio_test_challenger) | pct(0) }} low; scorecard {{ "%.2f" | format(m.models.calibration_ratio_test_champion) }}, about {{ (1 - m.models.calibration_ratio_test_champion) | pct(0) }} low). Section 10 shows how the policy copes.</p>
<h3>Segments</h3>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.segments }}</div>
<p class="note">New-to-lender clients have no internal history, so both models rank them less well; cash loans are the most under-predicted product. A segmented scorecard or segment-specific cut-offs are a natural next step.</p>
<h3>Why is an applicant declined?</h3>
<div class="fig">{{ F.shap }}</div>
<p>Repayment history and previous applications together carry {{ track | pct(0) }} of the challenger's total impact. Every decline comes with up to four principal reasons in plain English (SHAP for the challenger,
points lost for the scorecard), the format of an adverse-action notice. Example: application #{{ example.case_id }} ({{ example.product }}, {{ example.amount | num }} CU), challenger PD {{ example.pd | pct }} against a break-even of
{{ example.breakeven | pct }} → declined. Challenger reasons: <i>{{ example.reasons | join("; ") }}</i>. Scorecard reasons: <i>{{ example.sc_reasons | join("; ") }}</i>. (Actual outcome, known only in hindsight: {{ example.outcome }}.)</p>
<h3>Champion scorecard variables</h3>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.scorecard }}</div>
<h3>Class imbalance: why we did not resample</h3>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.imbalance }}</div>
<p class="note">Re-weighting and SMOTE leave the ranking (AUC) about the same but inflate the output far above the true default rate. Fed into the break-even rule, those outputs decline most good customers; after recalibration they behave
like the unweighted model, so resampling added nothing but a calibration step (and SMOTE invents applicants that never existed). Decision: train on the true class mix, calibrate, and set the cut-off from costs.</p>
</section>

<section id="cutoff">
<h2>5 · Cost matrix and cut-off</h2>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.breakeven }}</div>
<div class="fig">{{ F.breakeven }}</div>
<p class="note">Approving a loan with predicted default probability p is worth (1 − p) × margin − p × loss; it pays only below the break-even PD = margin ÷ (margin + loss). The break-even moves most with the margin assumption,
which is why it is set per product.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.loss_view }}</div>
<h3>Cut-off table (challenger, single PD cut-offs)</h3>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.cutoff }}</div>
<h3>The cost-based rule by product</h3>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.by_product }}</div>
<p class="note">Thin-margin point-of-sale loans are tightened most; cash loans earn enough to absorb a high PD, so almost all are approved even though they default most often. This is why the business's count-based bad rate and
the money view can disagree.</p>
<h3>Sensitivity to the margin assumptions</h3>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.sens }}</div>
</section>

<section id="room">
<h2>6 · Question 1: is there room on the current cut-off?</h2>
<p>Status quo: the historical policy, which approved every loan in the data. Applying the cost-based rule (challenger) would decline:</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.room }}</div>
<p>Declining this tail is worth {{ m.room[CH].net_gain | money }} over the quarter (modelled credit losses avoided minus margin forgone). Tightening is fully observable. Loosening is not: the room created can be "spent" on new approvals
only if they are less risky than the declined tail, and their bad rate is unknown.</p>
<div class="fig">{{ F.headroom }}</div>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.headroom }}</div>
</section>

<section id="swap">
<h2>7 · Question 2: who swaps in and out with a better model?</h2>
<p>Status quo for this comparison: the champion scorecard applying the cost-based rule (approval rate {{ m.model_value.status_quo.approval_rate | pct }}, bad rate {{ m.model_value.status_quo.bad_rate_approved | pct(2) }}).
Both models score the same booked loans, so swap-ins and swap-outs are both observed. From 80% to just under 100% approval, the challenger's approved book has a lower bad rate; both equal the full book at 100%.</p>
<div class="fig">{{ F.tradeoff }}</div>
<h3>Challenger at the same approval rate</h3><div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.swap_same_rate }}</div>
<h3>Challenger at the same bad rate</h3><div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.swap_same_bad }}</div>
<h3>Challenger at its own cost-based rule (recommended)</h3><div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.swap_rule }}</div>
<div class="fig">{{ F.swap }}</div>
<p><b>Reading the swap sets.</b> Against the champion's rule, the recommended rule approves {{ m.model_value.rule_vs_rule.approval_rate_change | pp }} applicants and changes the approved book's bad rate by
{{ m.model_value.rule_vs_rule.bad_rate_change | pp(2) }}. It swaps out {{ SW.out_n | num }} loans that defaulted at {{ SW.out_rate | pct }} and swaps in {{ SW.in_n | num }} that defaulted at {{ SW.in_rate | pct }}
(95% interval for the gap: {{ (U['swap_out_minus_swap_in_bad_rate'][0] * 100) | round(1) }} to {{ (U['swap_out_minus_swap_in_bad_rate'][1] * 100) | round(1) }} points). Both groups are mostly point-of-sale loans
({{ SW.in_pos | pct(0) }} and {{ SW.out_pos | pct(0) }}), but the swap-ins include {{ SW.in_new | pct(0) }} new-to-lender clients against {{ SW.out_new | pct(0) }} of the swap-outs, and are smaller loans (median
{{ SW.in_med | num }} vs {{ SW.out_med | num }} CU). The other two comparisons show the same edge used differently: at the scorecard's approval rate the challenger cuts modelled credit losses by
{{ (-m.model_value.same_approval_rate.credit_loss_change_pct) | pct(0) }}; at the scorecard's bad rate it approves {{ m.model_value.same_bad_rate.extra_approvals_pp | pp }} more. These comparisons were matched after the fact on booked test loans. Each needs its own cut-off fixed in advance and its own test.</p>
