<section id="monitoring">
<h2>10 · Calibration drift and monitoring</h2>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.drift }}</div>
<p class="note">Refitting only the intercept on the test quarter (a hindsight check) brings the calibration ratio to 1.00 by construction, and the challenger's calibration slope on the test quarter is {{ "%.2f" | format(m.calibration_slope_test) }}: this suggests mainly a level shift, but does not prove the spread is right everywhere; decile and group gaps remain. The stress line shows the rule if every applicant's odds of default were 25% higher.</p>
<div class="fig">{{ F.psi }}</div>
<div class="fig">{{ F.calratio }}</div>
<div class="fig">{{ F.gini }}</div>
<div class="tbl-wrap">{{ T.csi }}</div>
<p><b>What the months after April 2020 teach.</b> The challenger's score distribution barely moved (PSI at most {{ "%.2f" | format(psi_ch_max) }}; the scorecard's reached {{ "%.2f" | format(psi_cp_max) }}, in step with the employment
field's collapse) while the bad rate halved and predicted PDs ran well above actual; Gini held. Score stability alone would have missed the change. The drop is a step in April 2020 and then flat. It may reflect
COVID-era measures, the lender's own tightening or loans too recent to have defaulted (the outcome horizon is not disclosed); the data cannot separate these, so the back-test is illustrative. Retrospective feature-level checks show a data-capture break (the employment field's fill rate collapsed in April to May 2020). The plan therefore pairs monthly applicant-mix checks with calibration on mature vintages and an
early outcome (30+ days past due once vintages reach month 3 on book); detection speed was not back-tested. The back-test is not blind: two employment fields were dropped partly because their collection stopped in June 2020, and the inputs that had
already shifted between training and test were added to the feature-stability list.</p>
