<section id="limits">
<h2>12 · Limitations and next steps</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Selection bias.</b> Only approved applicants have outcomes; approval rates are shares of the approved population and the bad rate of past rejects is unknown (reject inference). The below-cut-off test addresses this.</li>
  <li><b>Assumptions.</b> Margins, loss given default and review effectiveness are not in the data (<code>docs/assumptions.md</code>); money figures move with them, the ranking of policies does not.</li>
  <li><b>Calibration.</b> PDs were calibrated on November to December 2019 and under-predict the test quarter; recalibrate on the most recent mature vintage before use.</li>
  <li><b>Target.</b> The default definition and horizon are not disclosed; vintages up to March 2020 are treated as mature. No vintage curves (months on book) can be built from a single default flag.</li>
  <li><b>Correlation, not causation.</b> Segment differences and SHAP reasons describe how risk and inputs move together in approved loans; they are not causal effects.</li>
  <li><b>Fairness scope.</b> Only sex and age are available; other protected characteristics cannot be tested.</li>
</ul>
